\begin{rehearsal}
\pnote{The song} An evening prayer for the household, printed in Kraków in
the early 1550s. Luther’s Small Catechism taught children to commend
themselves at bedtime into God’s hands and to ask for his angel against the
enemy. Polish Lutherans translated that prayer, and Trzecieski’s stanzas
3 and 4 follow it almost clause by clause. His first stanza recalls Compline,
the old night office.

\pnote{The words} Sing the old forms as printed (\pl{zmierzka, naszem
strażem, nawięcej, wszytki, swoję, Jesu}) with modern pronunciation; the
series pronunciation guide gives the sounds and the historical options.
\pl{Ześli} in stanza 3 means ‘send’.

\pnote{Stanzas} All four go to the same music, and most performances sing
one or two. Stanzas 1 and 2 are under the notes, each with its own underlay
where the words need it, so that the long runs fall on the words that matter.
The parts do not always move to a new syllable together; keep them apart where
the score has them apart. For stanzas 3 and 4, give each syllable the notes
its counterpart has in stanza 1.

\pnote{Pitch and voices} The score is a minor third above the print, for
sopranos, altos, tenors and basses. The Tenor carries the hymn tune, one note
to each syllable; the other voices move around it. To sing at the printed
pitch (men with a high Cantus, or low voices), use the critical edition.

\pnote{Tempo} A minim of about 66–80, felt two to the semibreve and four to
the bar. Keep the semiminim runs light.

\pnote{The score} Note values are those of the 1550s print. A bar is one
breve. The light bar lines run between the staves only, so a note that
crosses one is held for its full value. Accidentals above the staff are
editorial; the bracketed flat in bar 29 is optional.
\end{rehearsal}
\footcolophon
